    "lawCode": "Amd §1891, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7704\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to\ndistribution utility\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo expand the definition of distribution utility to include a rural\nelectric cooperative, thereby allowing them to participate in the\nGreen Jobs Green New York program established in 2009 and administered\nby the New York State Energy Research and Development Authority\n(NYSERDA) with a goal of making 1,000,000 homes, businesses, and\nnot-for-profits in New York more energy efficient while creating\nthousands of new green jobs and training the workers to fill them.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1.\n\nAmends the definition of distribution utility to include rural\nelectric cooperative.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nGreen Jobs-Green New York promotes the widespread dissemination of\nenergy conservation and clean energy technologies, providing a clear\nand cost effective pathway for communities in New York State to\ncurtail emissions of greenhouse gases and harmful air contaminants,\nreduce dependence on fossil fuels, lower housing costs, support\ncommunity development and create green jobs to sustain and enhance our\neconomy.\n\nRural Electric Cooperatives were established in the early 1940's to\nprovide electric service to rural areas. They serve over 70,000 New\nYork residents and businesses throughout Cattaraugus, Chautauqua,\nChenango, Delaware, Herkimer, Madison, Oneida, Otsego, Schoharie and\nSteuben Counties.\n\nCurrently, this legislation provides competitive grants for outreach,\nenrollment and related services to implement this program. Eligible\nentities include distribution utilities, which as currently defined\nunder the Green Jobs Statute does not include Rural Electric\nCooperatives. This legislation merely adds rural electric cooperatives\nto the definition of distribution facility.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2010: New\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
